<chapter xml:id="ch-codebook">
  <title>The Codebook</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The Episode Model describes the local justification steps visible in a response. A
      codebook connects that response-level evidence to the broader control structures
      the project studies. This connection cannot be made from isolated words alone. A
      possible code must be interpreted relative to the exact question, the mathematical
      state supplied to the student, and the judgment the response is attempting to
      support.
    </p>

    <p>
      The project therefore separates two related resources:
      <dl width="medium">
        <li>
          <title>Control-structure library</title>
          <p>
            A reusable catalog defining the control structures recognized by the
            project.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Question-specific codebook</title>
          <p>
            The controls selected for one question, together with the criteria,
            judgments, and inferential links through which each control may appear
            in that question.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      The distinction is essential because a control structure may recur across 
      questions, but the inference it implies depends on the question. The shared 
      library provides a catalog of reusable control structures, while the question-
      specific codebook provides the mathematical anchors needed for matching and 
      correctness evaluation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-codebook-purpose">
    <title>Purpose of the Codebook</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-library">
      <title>Control-Structure Library</title>

      <p>
        At the library level, each control structure has one reusable
        identity rather than a separate definition for every question on which it
        appears. A control such as reasoning from pivots, consistency, dimension, or a
        free variable can therefore be studied across questions without treating each
        occurrence as a new category.
      </p>

      <p>
        The library records what a control structure <em>is</em>. At minimum, it provides:
        <ul>
          <li>a stable code identifier and name;</li>
          <li>a concise definition of the control;</li>
          <li>the mathematical decision or validation activity it represents;</li>
          <li>inclusion and exclusion guidance;</li>
          <li>positive and near-miss examples; and</li>
          <li>relationships to easily confused controls.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The library definition must be broad enough to remain meaningful across
        questions. It should not encode one question's correct criterion-to-judgment
        link as though that link were universally valid. Question-specific scope and
        correctness belong in the working codebook described next.
      </p>

      <p>
        A shared library reduces duplication, but it does not remove interpretation.
        Its purpose is to give researchers common names and common boundaries so that
        their interpretations can be compared, discussed, and revised. A code remains
        an analytic category, not a keyword label.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-working">
      <title>Question-Specific Codebook</title>

      <p>
        A <term>codebook</term> is a subset of library controls selected for a particular
        question. For each selected control, it records how that control can operate in 
        the question's mathematical context. It therefore binds a shared code identity to 
        question-specific information.
      </p>

      <p>
        The broader context may be summarized by a question-level analytic frame
        <md>
          K_q=(P_q,R_q,L_q,\Sigma_q),
        </md>
        where:
      </p>

      <tabular valign="top">
        <col width="5%"/>
        <col width="85%"/>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>P_q</m>:</p></cell>
          <cell><p>
              <term>Question &amp; task context</term>. The exact prompt, supplied 
              conditions, requested outputs, and mathematical situation for question 
              <m>q</m>.
            </p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>R_q</m>:</p></cell>
          <cell><p>
              <term>relevant operations</term>. The mathematical actions that may 
              produce or transform objects used in a response.
            </p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>L_q</m>:</p></cell>
          <cell><p>
              <term>representation system</term>. The symbolic, linguistic, graphical, 
              or technological representations available in and around the question.
            </p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>\Sigma_q</m>:</p></cell>
          <cell><p>
              <term>relevant controls</term>. The control structures that may reasonably 
              be applied when evaluating results or making decisions for this question.
            </p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        This frame is a researcher's description of the question context (not the student's 
        conception). For example, the statement <q>a free variable exists</q> can support 
        the judgment that a solution is not unique when the system is known to be
        consistent. If consistency has not been established, the same criterion does not
        by itself show that solutions exist. The library identity for a free-variable
        control remains stable, but the working codebook must state the conditions under
        which a particular inference is valid.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-entry">
      <title>A Codebook Entry</title>

      <p>
        A useful codebook entry gives a reviewer enough information to distinguish the
        intended control from nearby alternatives. The following fields describe the
        project's canonical content model. The exact schema may use different column names, 
        but distinctions should be the same.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-codebook-entry-anatomy">
        <title>Recommended contents of a control-structure entry.</title>
        <tabular top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="25%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="75%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Purpose</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Identifier</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A stable and unique code ID.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Name</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A short human-readable name.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Definition</p></cell>
            <cell><p>State the broader control or decision rule without tying it to one question.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Inclusion rules</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Describe the evidence that supports assigning the code.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Exclusion rules</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Identify reasoning patterns that resemble the intended control, but are not instances of it.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><m>\text{Surface markers}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Common words or phrases that may help identify codes. These are search aids only.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Question-specific bindings are not part of the universal library definition.
        They are added when the entry is selected for a working codebook. This prevents
        a correct inference for one question from being silently reused where it is not
        valid.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-codebook-normative-links">
    <title>Question-Specific Information</title>

    <p>
      Before coding responses to question <m>q</m>, the research team establishes the
      mathematical reference against which episodes will be interpreted. This work
      extends the question-level analytic context introduced in
      <xref ref="subsec-framework-question-frame" text="title"/> and supplies the
      normative reference used by the correctness overlay in
      <xref ref="subsec-episode-normative-reference" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-criteria">
      <title>Criteria</title>

      <p>
        The codebook identifies mathematical features that may function as
        criteria in responses to the question. These may include observations,
        conditions, intermediate results, comparisons, definitions, or cited theorems.
        For each criterion, the team should record:
        <ul>
          <li>a concise canonical description;</li>
          <li>the conditions under which the criterion is true;</li>
          <li>common student phrasings or representations;</li>
          <li>nearby features that should not be treated as equivalent; and</li>
          <li>the controls for which the criterion may provide evidence.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A list of criteria is not a list of keywords. The same word may refer to
        different mathematical features, and the same feature may be expressed through
        different words. A mention of <q>pivot</q>, for example, could report an
        operation, identify a count, compare pivots with variables, or support a
        dimension argument. Coding depends on the role the feature plays in the episode.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-judgments">
      <title>Judgments and Question Goals</title>

      <p>
        The codebook also records judgments a response may draw in the question
        context. A judgment is a conclusion, classification, or decision within an
        episode. Examples include statements about consistency, uniqueness, span,
        dimension, monotonicity, or the number of solutions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Judgments must be distinguished from the full set of goals in the prompt. A
        question may ask a student to make several determinations, identify specific
        objects, or explain multiple parts of an answer. The project therefore records:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Episode judgments</title>
            <p>The local conclusions that may appear in individual episodes.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Question goals</title>
            <p>
              The complete set of requested outcomes against which response-level goal
              coverage is evaluated.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        A response may contain one or more mathematically sound episodes while leaving 
        part of the question unanswered. Conversely, a response may state the requested 
        final judgment without providing a criterion that reveals how it was reached.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-links">
      <title>Valid and Invalid Inferential Links</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>\mathcal{T}_q</m> denote the question-specific set of inferential links
        accepted as mathematically valid for question <m>q</m>. An entry in
        <m>\mathcal{T}_q</m> states that, under the question's supplied conditions, a
        particular criterion validly supports a particular judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        For every control, the codebook should record at least one criterion-to-judgment 
        relationship. It should also document common invalid or incomplete links when 
        they are likely to be confused with the valid relationship. These near-misses are 
        important because a student may cite a true criterion but connect it to a judgment 
        through an invalid justification.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, in a known consistent system, the link
        <md>
          \text{a free variable exists}
          \Longrightarrow
          \text{the solution is not unique}
        </md>
        is valid. The broader claim
        <md>
          \text{there are more variables than equations}
          \Longrightarrow
          \text{there are infinitely many solutions}
        </md>
        is not valid without information about the system's consistency. A useful 
        codebook records both the valid link and the tempting overgeneralization.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-codebook-governance">
    <title>Vocabulary and Codebook Tracking</title>

    <p>
      The codebook and controlled vocabulary are related but distinct resources. The
      <term>controlled vocabulary</term> contains canonical propositions used to
      normalize recurring mathematical language, such as <c>free_var_exists</c> or
      <c>system_consistent</c>. The codebook contains analytic categories and rules for
      deciding when those propositions use a control structure. Vocabulary terms help
      represent an episode; codebook entries classify the role of that episode.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-versioning">
      <title>Versioning and Freezing</title>

      <p>
        Every research coding record must identify the codebook and vocabulary versions
        under which it was produced. A version records a stable set of definitions,
        identifiers, question bindings, and normative links. When a substantive rule or
        definition changes, the project creates a new version rather than silently
        rewriting the version used by earlier coding.
      </p>

      <p>
        Current version labels belong in the compact project version summary described
        in <xref ref="sec-version-summary" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-codebook-oov">
      <title>Out-of-Vocabulary Terms</title>

      <p>
        An <term>out-of-vocabulary</term> or <term>OOV</term> case contains relevant
        mathematical language or a proposition not represented in the current controlled
        vocabulary. The raw student wording must be preserved. It should not be replaced
        immediately with the nearest familiar term, because the wording may indicate:
        <ul>
          <li>a new expression of an existing proposition;</li>
          <li>a missing synonym or representation;</li>
          <li>a genuinely new criterion or control structure;</li>
          <li>reasoning imported from another source;</li>
          <li>incomplete or ambiguous reasoning; or</li>
          <li>reasoning outside the project's current scope.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        OOV and no-match are not synonyms. An OOV case exposes a gap in the vocabulary
        used to represent the response. A <term>no-match</term> case may use familiar
        vocabulary but contain an episode that no current codebook entry describes
        adequately. The first suggests a representation problem; the second suggests a
        classification or scope problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        A single unusual response does not automatically justify a new vocabulary term
        or code. Reviewers should preserve the case, compare it with similar responses,
        and decide whether to add a synonym, accept a new proposition, create a new
        control, revise an existing entry, or retain the case as outside scope. Detailed
        response-level actions appear in
        <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-codebook-summary">
    <title>Codebook Architecture Summary</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-codebook-architecture-summary">
      <title>Canonical distinctions in the codebook architecture.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="24%"/>
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="44%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Resource</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What it contains</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What it does not determine alone</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Shared library</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Stable code identities, names, definitions, and general boundaries.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Whether the control is in scope or correct for a particular question.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Working codebook</p></cell>
          <cell><p>In-scope controls and their question-specific normative bindings.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The final interpretation of an ambiguous student response.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Controlled vocabulary</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Canonical propositions and recognized expressions.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Which control structure an episode enacts.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>\mathcal{T}_q</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Valid criterion-to-judgment links for question <m>q</m>.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Whether the criterion is true in the actual student work.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Question goals</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The outcomes a complete response should address.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Whether each local episode is mathematically sound.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The shared library provides stable control-structure identities; the codebook 
      selects and interprets those controls for one question; the controlled vocabulary 
      represents recurring propositions; and the normative link set records which
      criterion-to-judgment relationships are valid. Versioning, human acceptance, and
      preservation of OOV and no-match cases keep these resources auditable as the
      project develops.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
